    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The land that became Coto de Caza was originally part of Rancho Mission Viejo.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coto_de_Caza%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Amoco Oil Company turned the early 20th‑century ranch into a hunting preserve before it evolved into a master‑planned community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/coto-de-caza-29653"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Planning began in 1968 with a vision for a hunting lodge; the community officially opened in 2003 after decades of development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coto_de_Caza%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Los Ranchos Estates, a 355‑acre rural enclave of 75 custom homes, sits behind Coto de Caza’s gated entrance.",
